  "notes": "The lane's most technically virtuosic voice, and the entry makes vocal athleticism the load-bearing parameter: \"wide range crossing the break, long melismatic runs placed at phrase ends, sustained belted climax notes, and improvised riffing over the outro — so model very high note-density in the final third of the song and low density at the start\". A single model cannot ramp density by section, so the range is authored wide (4–10) with `endVariation` and a raised octave jump carrying the runs, and the arrangement gives the ad-lib vamp its own long outro. The band is the catalogue's own 75–106 and the keys are the named F minor and G minor with D♭, A♭ and G major on the up-tempos. Drums are the stated live kit: \"snare crisp on 2&4 with ghost strokes on the surrounding 16ths, ride-led on the slow material, hats in swung 16ths on the mid-tempos, and a full stop-time break before the last chorus\" — hence the high ghost probability across six positions, the ride lane, the 16th-weighted hat base and `fillBeforeSection`. Harmony is gospel-jazz \"ii–V–I with tritone subs, minor-line-cliché descents, diminished passing chords into the IV\", so both ii–V families are authored and `susOrDimProb` is raised. Bass is \"walking, gospel-style thumb motion and chromatic approach into every chord change; the bass is a countermelodic voice, not a pad\". ⚠ The INFERRED parameters are all drum probabilities and velocities and all melody numbers; the research marks its harmonic detail as idiom inference. ⚠ The 119–190 readings are doubled [LOW-CONF] and the folded band is encoded.",
